1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,950
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,130 --> 00:00:03,646
Here's a question that tells you a

3
00:00:03,646 --> 00:00:04,359
lot about money.

4
00:00:04,539 --> 00:00:07,316
Why do so many genuinely rich people

5
00:00:07,316 --> 00:00:09,090
drive old, boring cars?

6
00:00:09,270 --> 00:00:11,120
You'd think the people who can most

7
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:12,971
afford a brand new luxury car would

8
00:00:12,971 --> 00:00:14,239
be the ones buying them.

9
00:00:14,419 --> 00:00:15,854
But again and again,

10
00:00:15,854 --> 00:00:19,440
researchers find millionaires driving ten year old

11
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:22,309
sedans, while people with far less money

12
00:00:22,309 --> 00:00:24,102
drive the shiny new SUVs.

13
00:00:24,282 --> 00:00:26,142
Today, we'll figure out why,

14
00:00:26,142 --> 00:00:29,530
through fifteen money rules from real millionaires.

15
00:00:29,879 --> 00:00:31,575
Here's the research that started it.

16
00:00:31,755 --> 00:00:32,949
In Stop Acting Rich,

17
00:00:32,949 --> 00:00:35,638
Thomas Stanley reported that the median price

18
00:00:35,638 --> 00:00:38,446
millionaires paid for their most recent vehicle

19
00:00:38,446 --> 00:00:40,716
was about thirty one thousand dollars,

20
00:00:40,716 --> 00:00:42,628
based on surveys in the late two

21
00:00:42,628 --> 00:00:43,225
thousands.

22
00:00:43,405 --> 00:00:46,049
Meanwhile, the average new car today sells

23
00:00:46,049 --> 00:00:47,875
for more than fifty thousand.

24
00:00:48,224 --> 00:00:50,290
Those numbers come from different years,

25
00:00:50,290 --> 00:00:52,149
so they're not a perfect comparison.

26
00:00:52,329 --> 00:00:54,035
But the pattern is striking.

27
00:00:54,215 --> 00:00:57,569
Stanley found that many millionaires bought used

28
00:00:57,569 --> 00:00:59,386
cars, kept them for years,

29
00:00:59,386 --> 00:01:01,971
and chose reliable mainstream brands.

30
00:01:02,151 --> 00:01:03,667
He also found that a lot of

31
00:01:03,667 --> 00:01:06,530
the people driving luxury cars weren't millionaires

32
00:01:06,530 --> 00:01:06,922
at all.

33
00:01:07,102 --> 00:01:09,146
They were high earners spending their income

34
00:01:09,146 --> 00:01:10,028
on looking wealthy.

35
00:01:10,208 --> 00:01:12,343
That gap between looking rich and being

36
00:01:12,343 --> 00:01:14,094
rich is what this whole video is

37
00:01:14,094 --> 00:01:14,423
about.

38
00:01:14,772 --> 00:01:17,351
We'll go through fifteen rules from three

39
00:01:17,351 --> 00:01:17,791
angles.

40
00:01:17,971 --> 00:01:19,492
First, the numbers.

41
00:01:19,672 --> 00:01:22,496
The car math that millionaires understand and

42
00:01:22,496 --> 00:01:23,689
most people ignore.

43
00:01:23,869 --> 00:01:25,796
Second, the psychology.

44
00:01:25,976 --> 00:01:27,856
Why rich people don't need a car

45
00:01:27,856 --> 00:01:28,972
to feel successful.

46
00:01:29,152 --> 00:01:30,742
And third, the playbook.

47
00:01:30,922 --> 00:01:32,688
The rules they apply not just to

48
00:01:32,688 --> 00:01:34,510
cars, but to everything they buy.

49
00:01:34,690 --> 00:01:35,376
By the end,

50
00:01:35,376 --> 00:01:37,123
you'll see why an old car in

51
00:01:37,123 --> 00:01:39,618
a millionaire's driveway isn't a sign of

52
00:01:39,618 --> 00:01:40,367
being cheap.

53
00:01:40,547 --> 00:01:42,335
It's a sign of a strategy.

54
00:01:42,684 --> 00:01:44,426
Let's begin with the numbers.

55
00:01:44,606 --> 00:01:46,713
Rule number one is the one that

56
00:01:46,713 --> 00:01:48,344
changes everything else.

57
00:01:48,695 --> 00:01:49,600
Our sources.

58
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:52,850
Millionaire car data comes from Thomas Stanley's

59
00:01:52,850 --> 00:01:53,426
research.

60
00:01:53,606 --> 00:01:56,019
New car prices come from Kelley Blue

61
00:01:56,019 --> 00:01:58,767
Book, loan and payment data from Edmunds,

62
00:01:58,767 --> 00:02:00,711
and the full cost of owning a

63
00:02:00,711 --> 00:02:03,393
car from AAA's Your Driving Costs study.

64
00:02:03,573 --> 00:02:06,087
The average age of cars on American

65
00:02:06,087 --> 00:02:08,313
roads comes from S and P Global

66
00:02:08,313 --> 00:02:08,960
Mobility.

67
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:11,752
Ideas on the psychology of money come

68
00:02:11,752 --> 00:02:14,788
from Morgan Housel's book The Psychology of

69
00:02:14,788 --> 00:02:18,036
Money and from research by psychologist Thomas

70
00:02:18,036 --> 00:02:18,672
Gilovich.

71
00:02:18,852 --> 00:02:21,890
Growth examples use a stated seven percent

72
00:02:21,890 --> 00:02:23,482
average yearly return,

73
00:02:23,482 --> 00:02:25,725
an illustration, not a promise.

74
00:02:25,905 --> 00:02:27,287
This is education,

75
00:02:27,287 --> 00:02:28,900
not financial advice.

76
00:02:29,251 --> 00:02:30,040
Angle one.

77
00:02:30,220 --> 00:02:30,917
The numbers.

78
00:02:31,097 --> 00:02:33,354
Five rules about the math of cars,

79
00:02:33,354 --> 00:02:35,878
and why millionaires take that math so

80
00:02:35,878 --> 00:02:36,542
seriously.

81
00:02:36,892 --> 00:02:38,018
Rule number one.

82
00:02:38,198 --> 00:02:40,304
Let someone else pay for the biggest

83
00:02:40,304 --> 00:02:41,123
drop in value.

84
00:02:41,474 --> 00:02:43,846
A new car loses value fastest in

85
00:02:43,846 --> 00:02:45,328
its first few years.

86
00:02:45,508 --> 00:02:48,580
Sources like Carfax and Edmunds estimate that

87
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:51,311
many new cars lose around twenty percent

88
00:02:51,311 --> 00:02:53,564
of their value in the first year,

89
00:02:53,564 --> 00:02:56,022
and roughly half or more within five

90
00:02:56,022 --> 00:02:58,070
years, depending on the model.

91
00:02:58,250 --> 00:03:00,479
That loss is called depreciation,

92
00:03:00,479 --> 00:03:02,707
and it's one of the biggest costs

93
00:03:02,707 --> 00:03:03,788
of owning a car.

94
00:03:03,968 --> 00:03:05,619
It never shows up on a bill,

95
00:03:05,619 --> 00:03:07,741
so most people never think about it.

96
00:03:07,921 --> 00:03:10,201
But it's real money leaving your pocket

97
00:03:10,201 --> 00:03:11,195
every single day.

98
00:03:11,546 --> 00:03:14,170
Millionaires who understand this often buy cars

99
00:03:14,170 --> 00:03:15,957
that are two or three years old.

100
00:03:16,137 --> 00:03:18,336
By then, the first owner has absorbed

101
00:03:18,336 --> 00:03:20,120
the steepest part of the drop.

102
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:22,561
The car is often still reliable,

103
00:03:22,561 --> 00:03:24,965
may still have some warranty left,

104
00:03:24,965 --> 00:03:27,509
and costs thousands of dollars less.

105
00:03:27,689 --> 00:03:29,618
On a fifty thousand dollar car,

106
00:03:29,618 --> 00:03:32,230
letting someone else take the first year's

107
00:03:32,230 --> 00:03:34,843
hit could easily save ten thousand dollars

108
00:03:34,843 --> 00:03:35,340
or more.

109
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:37,968
That's money that can be invested instead

110
00:03:37,968 --> 00:03:39,700
of evaporating in a driveway.

111
00:03:40,050 --> 00:03:41,152
Apply the rule.

112
00:03:41,332 --> 00:03:42,929
Before your next purchase,

113
00:03:42,929 --> 00:03:44,710
look up the price of the same

114
00:03:44,710 --> 00:03:46,491
model brand new and at two or

115
00:03:46,491 --> 00:03:47,474
three years old.

116
00:03:47,654 --> 00:03:50,534
Compare the difference with what you'd actually

117
00:03:50,534 --> 00:03:52,496
lose in features or reliability.

118
00:03:52,676 --> 00:03:54,310
For many reliable models,

119
00:03:54,310 --> 00:03:56,663
the slightly used version is the far

120
00:03:56,663 --> 00:03:57,447
better deal.

121
00:03:57,627 --> 00:04:00,426
Consider certified pre owned programs if you

122
00:04:00,426 --> 00:04:02,016
want extra peace of mind.

123
00:04:02,366 --> 00:04:03,538
Rule number two.

124
00:04:03,718 --> 00:04:05,426
Keep your car for a long,

125
00:04:05,426 --> 00:04:06,110
long time.

126
00:04:06,460 --> 00:04:09,187
According to S and P Global Mobility,

127
00:04:09,187 --> 00:04:11,619
the average age of cars and light

128
00:04:11,619 --> 00:04:14,494
trucks on American roads has climbed to

129
00:04:14,494 --> 00:04:16,632
a record of over twelve and a

130
00:04:16,632 --> 00:04:17,443
half years.

131
00:04:17,623 --> 00:04:19,986
Modern cars, with good maintenance,

132
00:04:19,986 --> 00:04:22,416
can often last well over one hundred

133
00:04:22,416 --> 00:04:23,834
fifty thousand miles.

134
00:04:24,014 --> 00:04:26,461
Yet many people trade in every three

135
00:04:26,461 --> 00:04:27,413
to five years,

136
00:04:27,413 --> 00:04:30,133
starting a new cycle of depreciation and

137
00:04:30,133 --> 00:04:31,560
a new loan each time.

138
00:04:31,911 --> 00:04:33,869
Every time you trade in a car,

139
00:04:33,869 --> 00:04:34,848
you lose money.

140
00:04:35,028 --> 00:04:36,741
Depreciation, sales tax,

141
00:04:36,741 --> 00:04:39,738
dealer fees, higher insurance on the newer

142
00:04:39,738 --> 00:04:41,879
car, and often a new loan with

143
00:04:41,879 --> 00:04:42,807
new interest.

144
00:04:42,987 --> 00:04:45,387
Keeping a car for ten years instead

145
00:04:45,387 --> 00:04:47,651
of five can cut your lifetime car

146
00:04:47,651 --> 00:04:48,954
costs dramatically.

147
00:04:49,134 --> 00:04:52,167
Many millionaires drive a car until repairs

148
00:04:52,167 --> 00:04:54,777
genuinely cost more than replacement.

149
00:04:54,957 --> 00:04:56,443
Not until they get bored.

150
00:04:56,793 --> 00:04:57,988
Apply the rule.

151
00:04:58,168 --> 00:04:59,912
Set a goal to keep your current

152
00:04:59,912 --> 00:05:01,712
car at least five years past the

153
00:05:01,712 --> 00:05:02,499
final payment.

154
00:05:02,679 --> 00:05:04,780
Track repair costs in a notebook.

155
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:08,142
Replace the car when repairs become frequent,

156
00:05:08,142 --> 00:05:09,909
expensive and unreliable,

157
00:05:09,909 --> 00:05:12,100
not when a new model comes out.

158
00:05:12,451 --> 00:05:13,519
Rule number three.

159
00:05:13,699 --> 00:05:15,546
Know the full cost of owning a

160
00:05:15,546 --> 00:05:17,147
car, not just the payment.

161
00:05:17,497 --> 00:05:20,822
AAA's Your Driving Costs study estimated that

162
00:05:20,822 --> 00:05:23,483
owning and operating a new car costs

163
00:05:23,483 --> 00:05:26,440
more than twelve thousand dollars a year

164
00:05:26,440 --> 00:05:29,396
on average when you add up depreciation,

165
00:05:29,396 --> 00:05:31,539
insurance, fuel, maintenance,

166
00:05:31,539 --> 00:05:34,052
financing, registration and taxes.

167
00:05:34,232 --> 00:05:36,438
That's over a thousand dollars a month.

168
00:05:36,618 --> 00:05:38,886
Most people only think about the monthly

169
00:05:38,886 --> 00:05:40,871
payment, which is just one piece of

170
00:05:40,871 --> 00:05:41,494
that total.

171
00:05:41,845 --> 00:05:44,410
Millionaires tend to think in total cost.

172
00:05:44,590 --> 00:05:47,148
A cheaper, reliable car doesn't just have

173
00:05:47,148 --> 00:05:48,271
a smaller payment.

174
00:05:48,451 --> 00:05:50,660
It usually has lower insurance,

175
00:05:50,660 --> 00:05:53,439
lower registration fees in many states,

176
00:05:53,439 --> 00:05:55,149
cheaper tires and parts,

177
00:05:55,149 --> 00:05:56,717
and less depreciation.

178
00:05:56,897 --> 00:05:59,626
The full cost difference between a modest

179
00:05:59,626 --> 00:06:01,422
car and a luxury car can be

180
00:06:01,422 --> 00:06:03,619
many thousands of dollars a year,

181
00:06:03,619 --> 00:06:04,351
every year.

182
00:06:04,701 --> 00:06:05,745
Apply the rule.

183
00:06:05,925 --> 00:06:07,686
Add up what your car really costs

184
00:06:07,686 --> 00:06:08,433
you in a year.

185
00:06:08,613 --> 00:06:10,562
Payment, insurance, fuel,

186
00:06:10,562 --> 00:06:14,694
maintenance, registration, parking and an estimate of

187
00:06:14,694 --> 00:06:15,707
depreciation.

188
00:06:15,887 --> 00:06:17,327
Divide by twelve.

189
00:06:17,507 --> 00:06:19,738
That's the real monthly price of your

190
00:06:19,738 --> 00:06:19,979
car.

191
00:06:20,159 --> 00:06:21,125
Use that number,

192
00:06:21,125 --> 00:06:22,091
not the payment,

193
00:06:22,091 --> 00:06:24,084
when you decide on your next one.

194
00:06:24,434 --> 00:06:25,571
Rule number four.

195
00:06:25,751 --> 00:06:27,924
Pay cash for cars when you can,

196
00:06:27,924 --> 00:06:29,606
and keep any loan short.

197
00:06:29,956 --> 00:06:32,381
Edmunds has reported that many new car

198
00:06:32,381 --> 00:06:34,869
loans now stretch to seventy two months

199
00:06:34,869 --> 00:06:37,294
or more, with average new car payments

200
00:06:37,294 --> 00:06:39,592
above seven hundred dollars a month.

201
00:06:39,772 --> 00:06:42,272
Long loans mean more interest and more

202
00:06:42,272 --> 00:06:44,311
time owing more than the car is

203
00:06:44,311 --> 00:06:44,706
worth.

204
00:06:44,886 --> 00:06:47,568
Many quiet millionaires either pay cash or

205
00:06:47,568 --> 00:06:49,611
use short loans they can pay off

206
00:06:49,611 --> 00:06:50,122
quickly.

207
00:06:50,473 --> 00:06:53,062
There's a psychological benefit to cash too.

208
00:06:53,242 --> 00:06:54,886
When you have to hand over the

209
00:06:54,886 --> 00:06:56,202
full price, you feel it.

210
00:06:56,382 --> 00:06:58,600
That feeling makes you choose more carefully.

211
00:06:58,780 --> 00:07:01,101
A forty thousand dollar car feels very

212
00:07:01,101 --> 00:07:03,666
different as one payment from savings than

213
00:07:03,666 --> 00:07:05,560
as six hundred dollars a month.

214
00:07:05,740 --> 00:07:08,619
That's exactly why sellers push monthly payments.

215
00:07:08,969 --> 00:07:10,002
Apply the rule.

216
00:07:10,182 --> 00:07:11,529
If you need a loan,

217
00:07:11,529 --> 00:07:13,444
aim for four years or less,

218
00:07:13,444 --> 00:07:15,430
and make a big down payment.

219
00:07:15,610 --> 00:07:17,827
Better yet, start a car fund now.

220
00:07:18,007 --> 00:07:20,086
When your current car loan ends,

221
00:07:20,086 --> 00:07:22,814
keep making the same payment into savings.

222
00:07:22,994 --> 00:07:24,904
By the time you need another car,

223
00:07:24,904 --> 00:07:26,350
you may be able to buy it

224
00:07:26,350 --> 00:07:26,871
outright.

225
00:07:27,222 --> 00:07:28,452
Rule number five.

226
00:07:28,632 --> 00:07:29,921
Invest the difference.

227
00:07:30,271 --> 00:07:32,093
Here's where the numbers get exciting.

228
00:07:32,273 --> 00:07:34,465
Say one person drives a modest used

229
00:07:34,465 --> 00:07:36,718
car, and another has a seven hundred

230
00:07:36,718 --> 00:07:38,972
dollar monthly payment on a new one.

231
00:07:39,152 --> 00:07:41,439
If the first person invests that seven

232
00:07:41,439 --> 00:07:43,605
hundred dollars every month instead,

233
00:07:43,605 --> 00:07:46,071
at a seven percent average yearly return,

234
00:07:46,071 --> 00:07:48,177
after twenty years it could grow to

235
00:07:48,177 --> 00:07:50,704
over three hundred sixty thousand dollars.

236
00:07:51,055 --> 00:07:53,049
That's the secret behind the old car

237
00:07:53,049 --> 00:07:54,712
in the millionaire's driveway.

238
00:07:54,892 --> 00:07:57,062
It's not just about saving money on

239
00:07:57,062 --> 00:07:57,434
a car.

240
00:07:57,614 --> 00:07:59,948
It's about what the saved money becomes.

241
00:08:00,128 --> 00:08:01,034
Over a career,

242
00:08:01,034 --> 00:08:04,012
the difference between a car payment lifestyle

243
00:08:04,012 --> 00:08:06,083
and a paid off car lifestyle can

244
00:08:06,083 --> 00:08:09,061
easily reach hundreds of thousands of dollars,

245
00:08:09,061 --> 00:08:09,578
or more.

246
00:08:09,929 --> 00:08:10,950
Apply the rule.

247
00:08:11,130 --> 00:08:14,307
Calculate the difference between what you spend

248
00:08:14,307 --> 00:08:16,470
on your car and what a reliable,

249
00:08:16,470 --> 00:08:17,957
modest car would cost.

250
00:08:18,137 --> 00:08:20,294
Even if it's only two hundred dollars

251
00:08:20,294 --> 00:08:22,568
a month, set up an automatic investment

252
00:08:22,568 --> 00:08:23,501
for that amount.

253
00:08:23,681 --> 00:08:24,969
Watch what it becomes.

254
00:08:25,320 --> 00:08:26,817
Here's the math on screen.

255
00:08:26,997 --> 00:08:28,836
Seven hundred dollars a month,

256
00:08:28,836 --> 00:08:31,043
invested for twenty years at a seven

257
00:08:31,043 --> 00:08:32,881
percent average yearly return,

258
00:08:32,881 --> 00:08:35,394
grows to roughly three hundred sixty five

259
00:08:35,394 --> 00:08:36,436
thousand dollars.

260
00:08:36,616 --> 00:08:38,780
You'd put in one hundred sixty eight

261
00:08:38,780 --> 00:08:39,321
thousand.

262
00:08:39,672 --> 00:08:42,702
Let's compare two drivers over twenty years.

263
00:08:42,882 --> 00:08:45,200
Driver one buys a new fifty thousand

264
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:47,003
dollar car every five years,

265
00:08:47,003 --> 00:08:49,128
trading in the old one each time.

266
00:08:49,308 --> 00:08:50,597
That's four new cars.

267
00:08:50,777 --> 00:08:53,028
Each time, they lose a big chunk

268
00:08:53,028 --> 00:08:55,632
to depreciation, pay sales tax again,

269
00:08:55,632 --> 00:08:57,954
and usually start a new loan with

270
00:08:57,954 --> 00:08:58,869
new interest.

271
00:08:59,049 --> 00:09:01,046
They're never without a car payment.

272
00:09:01,396 --> 00:09:03,973
Driver two buys a reliable three year

273
00:09:03,973 --> 00:09:06,899
old car for around thirty thousand dollars

274
00:09:06,899 --> 00:09:08,501
and keeps it ten years.

275
00:09:08,681 --> 00:09:09,865
Then they do it again.

276
00:09:10,045 --> 00:09:11,949
That's two cars in twenty years.

277
00:09:12,129 --> 00:09:14,301
They pay much less depreciation,

278
00:09:14,301 --> 00:09:15,659
much less sales tax,

279
00:09:15,659 --> 00:09:17,966
and spend many of those years with

280
00:09:17,966 --> 00:09:19,188
no payment at all.

281
00:09:19,368 --> 00:09:21,663
They also pay less for insurance and

282
00:09:21,663 --> 00:09:23,385
registration along the way.

283
00:09:23,735 --> 00:09:26,132
The exact numbers depend on the cars,

284
00:09:26,132 --> 00:09:27,752
the loans and the market.

285
00:09:27,932 --> 00:09:29,894
But it's easy for the difference to

286
00:09:29,894 --> 00:09:32,362
reach well over one hundred thousand dollars

287
00:09:32,362 --> 00:09:34,213
in total costs over twenty years.

288
00:09:34,393 --> 00:09:35,561
Invested along the way,

289
00:09:35,561 --> 00:09:38,305
that difference could become several hundred thousand.

290
00:09:38,485 --> 00:09:40,854
Same need, getting from home to work.

291
00:09:41,034 --> 00:09:43,332
Completely different financial outcome.

292
00:09:43,683 --> 00:09:44,693
That's angle one.

293
00:09:44,873 --> 00:09:47,649
Let someone else pay the depreciation,

294
00:09:47,649 --> 00:09:48,964
keep it ten years,

295
00:09:48,964 --> 00:09:50,352
know the full cost,

296
00:09:50,352 --> 00:09:52,470
pay cash or keep loans short,

297
00:09:52,470 --> 00:09:54,370
and invest the difference.

298
00:09:54,550 --> 00:09:55,815
Now angle two.

299
00:09:55,995 --> 00:09:57,156
The psychology.

300
00:09:57,336 --> 00:09:58,938
Because the numbers are easy.

301
00:09:59,118 --> 00:10:00,728
The hard part is not caring what

302
00:10:00,728 --> 00:10:01,684
other people think.

303
00:10:02,034 --> 00:10:03,140
For many people,

304
00:10:03,140 --> 00:10:05,354
a car isn't just transportation.

305
00:10:05,534 --> 00:10:06,521
It's a statement.

306
00:10:06,701 --> 00:10:07,947
It says who you are,

307
00:10:07,947 --> 00:10:09,379
how successful you are,

308
00:10:09,379 --> 00:10:11,310
what kind of person you want to

309
00:10:11,310 --> 00:10:11,995
be seen as.

310
00:10:12,175 --> 00:10:15,554
Car companies spend enormous amounts on advertising

311
00:10:15,554 --> 00:10:18,005
that connects their cars with status,

312
00:10:18,005 --> 00:10:19,396
freedom and identity.

313
00:10:19,576 --> 00:10:22,165
Millionaires who drive old cars have learned

314
00:10:22,165 --> 00:10:24,813
to separate those feelings from the purchase.

315
00:10:25,163 --> 00:10:26,521
Rule number six.

316
00:10:26,701 --> 00:10:28,126
Your car is a tool,

317
00:10:28,126 --> 00:10:29,476
not your identity.

318
00:10:29,826 --> 00:10:32,949
Stanley found that many millionaires simply didn't

319
00:10:32,949 --> 00:10:34,261
care much about cars.

320
00:10:34,441 --> 00:10:36,116
They saw a car as a tool

321
00:10:36,116 --> 00:10:38,420
to get from one place to another,

322
00:10:38,420 --> 00:10:39,816
safely and reliably.

323
00:10:39,996 --> 00:10:42,160
Many found satisfaction elsewhere,

324
00:10:42,160 --> 00:10:43,306
in their families,

325
00:10:43,306 --> 00:10:46,808
their businesses, their communities and their financial

326
00:10:46,808 --> 00:10:47,635
independence.

327
00:10:47,815 --> 00:10:50,242
Their identity wasn't parked in the driveway.

328
00:10:50,592 --> 00:10:52,333
When your car is part of your

329
00:10:52,333 --> 00:10:54,915
identity, every few years you feel pressure

330
00:10:54,915 --> 00:10:55,756
to upgrade it,

331
00:10:55,756 --> 00:10:57,978
because the old one no longer matches

332
00:10:57,978 --> 00:10:59,299
who you think you are.

333
00:10:59,479 --> 00:11:01,162
When your car is just a tool,

334
00:11:01,162 --> 00:11:03,310
you replace it when it stops working.

335
00:11:03,490 --> 00:11:05,582
That one shift in thinking can save

336
00:11:05,582 --> 00:11:07,136
a fortune over a lifetime.

337
00:11:07,486 --> 00:11:08,542
Apply the rule.

338
00:11:08,722 --> 00:11:10,976
Ask yourself honestly what you want your

339
00:11:10,976 --> 00:11:12,159
car to say about you.

340
00:11:12,339 --> 00:11:14,348
Then ask whether there's a cheaper way

341
00:11:14,348 --> 00:11:15,299
to be that person.

342
00:11:15,479 --> 00:11:17,570
Usually, the things we want to be

343
00:11:17,570 --> 00:11:19,660
known for, like being successful,

344
00:11:19,660 --> 00:11:21,181
reliable or interesting,

345
00:11:21,181 --> 00:11:23,145
have nothing to do with what we

346
00:11:23,145 --> 00:11:23,525
drive.

347
00:11:23,875 --> 00:11:24,966
Rule number seven.

348
00:11:25,146 --> 00:11:27,363
Nobody is watching you as closely as

349
00:11:27,363 --> 00:11:27,979
you think.

350
00:11:28,329 --> 00:11:31,940
Psychologist Thomas Gilovich and his colleagues studied

351
00:11:31,940 --> 00:11:34,435
something called the spotlight effect.

352
00:11:34,615 --> 00:11:36,350
In one famous experiment,

353
00:11:36,350 --> 00:11:39,265
students wore an embarrassing T shirt into

354
00:11:39,265 --> 00:11:42,180
a room, and they greatly overestimated how

355
00:11:42,180 --> 00:11:43,776
many people noticed it.

356
00:11:43,956 --> 00:11:45,778
We all tend to believe others are

357
00:11:45,778 --> 00:11:47,821
paying much more attention to us than

358
00:11:47,821 --> 00:11:48,704
they really are.

359
00:11:49,055 --> 00:11:50,575
That applies to cars too.

360
00:11:50,755 --> 00:11:52,534
You might think people judge you for

361
00:11:52,534 --> 00:11:53,275
your old sedan.

362
00:11:53,455 --> 00:11:55,736
In reality, most people are too busy

363
00:11:55,736 --> 00:11:58,018
thinking about themselves to notice.

364
00:11:58,198 --> 00:12:00,887
Millionaires who drive old cars seem to

365
00:12:00,887 --> 00:12:02,818
understand this intuitively.

366
00:12:02,998 --> 00:12:04,920
They don't spend money performing for an

367
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:06,505
audience that isn't really there.

368
00:12:06,855 --> 00:12:07,888
Apply the rule.

369
00:12:08,068 --> 00:12:10,387
Next time you feel embarrassed about your

370
00:12:10,387 --> 00:12:12,594
car, ask yourself when you last noticed

371
00:12:12,594 --> 00:12:14,517
what a coworker or neighbor drove,

372
00:12:14,517 --> 00:12:16,553
and whether you thought less of them

373
00:12:16,553 --> 00:12:16,949
for it.

374
00:12:17,129 --> 00:12:18,081
Probably never.

375
00:12:18,261 --> 00:12:20,061
They're not thinking about yours either.

376
00:12:20,411 --> 00:12:21,363
Rule number eight.

377
00:12:21,543 --> 00:12:23,400
Real wealth is invisible.

378
00:12:23,751 --> 00:12:25,576
In The Psychology of Money,

379
00:12:25,576 --> 00:12:28,349
Morgan Housel describes what he calls the

380
00:12:28,349 --> 00:12:29,904
man in the car paradox.

381
00:12:30,084 --> 00:12:32,576
When you see someone driving a nice

382
00:12:32,576 --> 00:12:34,143
car, you rarely think,

383
00:12:34,143 --> 00:12:35,924
wow, that driver is cool.

384
00:12:36,104 --> 00:12:37,308
You think, wow,

385
00:12:37,308 --> 00:12:38,754
if I had that car,

386
00:12:38,754 --> 00:12:41,003
people would think I'm cool.

387
00:12:41,183 --> 00:12:43,341
The irony is that people who buy

388
00:12:43,341 --> 00:12:46,509
expensive things to earn admiration often don't

389
00:12:46,509 --> 00:12:49,880
get it, because observers are imagining themselves

390
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:50,622
in the car.

391
00:12:50,972 --> 00:12:53,265
Housel also points out that wealth is

392
00:12:53,265 --> 00:12:54,443
what you don't see.

393
00:12:54,623 --> 00:12:56,369
It's the cars not bought,

394
00:12:56,369 --> 00:12:57,905
the upgrades not made,

395
00:12:57,905 --> 00:12:59,580
the money left invested.

396
00:12:59,760 --> 00:13:01,591
A big house or a fancy car

397
00:13:01,591 --> 00:13:03,493
might mean someone is rich,

398
00:13:03,493 --> 00:13:05,887
or it might mean they're deeply in

399
00:13:05,887 --> 00:13:06,239
debt.

400
00:13:06,419 --> 00:13:08,230
You can't tell from the outside.

401
00:13:08,410 --> 00:13:10,419
Real wealth is the money still in

402
00:13:10,419 --> 00:13:11,150
the account.

403
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:12,440
Apply the rule.

404
00:13:12,620 --> 00:13:15,142
Next time you're tempted to buy something

405
00:13:15,142 --> 00:13:17,171
to impress, remember the paradox.

406
00:13:17,351 --> 00:13:18,617
People won't admire you.

407
00:13:18,797 --> 00:13:20,793
They'll imagine themselves with it.

408
00:13:20,973 --> 00:13:22,481
If you want respect,

409
00:13:22,481 --> 00:13:25,497
kindness and generosity work far better,

410
00:13:25,497 --> 00:13:26,929
and cost much less.

411
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:28,359
Rule number nine.

412
00:13:28,539 --> 00:13:29,561
Be willing to wait.

413
00:13:29,911 --> 00:13:32,561
In the famous Stanford marshmallow studies,

414
00:13:32,561 --> 00:13:34,779
children who could wait for a second

415
00:13:34,779 --> 00:13:37,121
treat instead of eating one right away

416
00:13:37,121 --> 00:13:38,908
were followed into adulthood.

417
00:13:39,088 --> 00:13:41,891
Later research has questioned how much those

418
00:13:41,891 --> 00:13:43,356
results really predict,

419
00:13:43,356 --> 00:13:46,032
and found that family background matters a

420
00:13:46,032 --> 00:13:46,286
lot.

421
00:13:46,466 --> 00:13:48,810
But the basic idea still resonates with

422
00:13:48,810 --> 00:13:50,553
how many millionaires behave.

423
00:13:50,733 --> 00:13:51,906
They're willing to wait.

424
00:13:52,256 --> 00:13:54,241
Driving an old car now so you

425
00:13:54,241 --> 00:13:56,773
can have financial freedom later is a

426
00:13:56,773 --> 00:13:58,827
form of delayed gratification.

427
00:13:59,007 --> 00:14:01,445
The new car feels good immediately.

428
00:14:01,625 --> 00:14:03,599
The investments feel good much later.

429
00:14:03,779 --> 00:14:06,533
People who build wealth have usually learned

430
00:14:06,533 --> 00:14:07,848
to enjoy the waiting,

431
00:14:07,848 --> 00:14:09,350
or at least to see it as

432
00:14:09,350 --> 00:14:10,164
a fair trade.

433
00:14:10,514 --> 00:14:11,535
Apply the rule.

434
00:14:11,715 --> 00:14:13,560
When you want a big purchase,

435
00:14:13,560 --> 00:14:14,896
set a waiting period.

436
00:14:15,076 --> 00:14:17,197
Thirty days for anything over a few

437
00:14:17,197 --> 00:14:19,500
hundred dollars, six months for a car.

438
00:14:19,680 --> 00:14:21,462
Use the time to save for it

439
00:14:21,462 --> 00:14:21,990
in cash.

440
00:14:22,170 --> 00:14:24,118
If you still want it after the

441
00:14:24,118 --> 00:14:26,001
wait, buy it with no regrets.

442
00:14:26,352 --> 00:14:27,814
Let's take a quick break.

443
00:14:27,994 --> 00:14:29,835
If you're enjoying these rules,

444
00:14:29,835 --> 00:14:31,617
subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

445
00:14:31,797 --> 00:14:33,927
It's free and it helps the channel

446
00:14:33,927 --> 00:14:35,996
grow, so we can keep making long,

447
00:14:35,996 --> 00:14:37,625
honest videos about money.

448
00:14:37,805 --> 00:14:39,278
Now, rule number ten,

449
00:14:39,278 --> 00:14:41,944
which explains why the new car feeling

450
00:14:41,944 --> 00:14:42,786
never lasts.

451
00:14:43,136 --> 00:14:44,227
Rule number ten.

452
00:14:44,407 --> 00:14:45,951
Get off the hedonic treadmill.

453
00:14:46,301 --> 00:14:49,328
Psychologists use the term hedonic adaptation,

454
00:14:49,328 --> 00:14:51,632
often called the hedonic treadmill,

455
00:14:51,632 --> 00:14:53,936
to describe how we quickly get used

456
00:14:53,936 --> 00:14:54,857
to new things.

457
00:14:55,037 --> 00:14:57,081
The thrill of a new car fades

458
00:14:57,081 --> 00:14:58,067
within months.

459
00:14:58,247 --> 00:14:59,710
It becomes just your car.

460
00:14:59,890 --> 00:15:02,305
Then you start wanting the next upgrade

461
00:15:02,305 --> 00:15:03,915
to feel that thrill again,

462
00:15:03,915 --> 00:15:05,277
and the cycle repeats.

463
00:15:05,628 --> 00:15:08,162
People who understand this stop chasing the

464
00:15:08,162 --> 00:15:09,459
thrill through things.

465
00:15:09,639 --> 00:15:13,229
Instead, they find longer lasting satisfaction in

466
00:15:13,229 --> 00:15:17,186
experiences, relationships, health and the security of

467
00:15:17,186 --> 00:15:18,579
having money saved.

468
00:15:18,759 --> 00:15:21,738
Research on happiness has often suggested that

469
00:15:21,738 --> 00:15:24,329
experiences and time with people we love

470
00:15:24,329 --> 00:15:26,920
bring more lasting joy than possessions.

471
00:15:27,271 --> 00:15:28,292
Apply the rule.

472
00:15:28,472 --> 00:15:30,546
Think about the last big purchase that

473
00:15:30,546 --> 00:15:31,201
excited you.

474
00:15:31,381 --> 00:15:33,540
How long did the excitement last?

475
00:15:33,720 --> 00:15:35,515
Use that answer the next time you're

476
00:15:35,515 --> 00:15:35,914
tempted.

477
00:15:36,094 --> 00:15:38,349
And consider spending that money on an

478
00:15:38,349 --> 00:15:40,425
experience you'll remember instead.

479
00:15:40,775 --> 00:15:44,155
There's one more psychological reason rich people

480
00:15:44,155 --> 00:15:45,604
often drive old cars.

481
00:15:45,784 --> 00:15:48,307
Once you have real financial security,

482
00:15:48,307 --> 00:15:50,962
you need less external proof of success.

483
00:15:51,142 --> 00:15:52,612
You know you're doing well,

484
00:15:52,612 --> 00:15:54,463
because your accounts tell you so.

485
00:15:54,643 --> 00:15:57,027
People who don't yet have that security

486
00:15:57,027 --> 00:15:59,473
often feel more pressure to show success

487
00:15:59,473 --> 00:16:00,390
through things.

488
00:16:00,740 --> 00:16:02,551
This creates a strange loop.

489
00:16:02,731 --> 00:16:04,720
The people who most need to save

490
00:16:04,720 --> 00:16:06,647
are often the ones who feel the

491
00:16:06,647 --> 00:16:08,698
most pressure to spend on status.

492
00:16:08,878 --> 00:16:10,948
And the people who've already saved feel

493
00:16:10,948 --> 00:16:11,932
the least pressure.

494
00:16:12,112 --> 00:16:13,625
Breaking that loop early,

495
00:16:13,625 --> 00:16:15,865
by caring less about what others see,

496
00:16:15,865 --> 00:16:17,862
is one of the most powerful money

497
00:16:17,862 --> 00:16:19,194
moves anyone can make.

498
00:16:19,543 --> 00:16:22,569
Many millionaires describe a quiet satisfaction in

499
00:16:22,569 --> 00:16:24,869
knowing that they could buy the luxury

500
00:16:24,869 --> 00:16:26,382
car, and choosing not to.

501
00:16:26,562 --> 00:16:28,130
That feeling of freedom,

502
00:16:28,130 --> 00:16:29,307
of having options,

503
00:16:29,307 --> 00:16:31,398
is often worth more to them than

504
00:16:31,398 --> 00:16:32,378
the car itself.

505
00:16:32,558 --> 00:16:36,050
Financial independence is the ultimate status symbol,

506
00:16:36,050 --> 00:16:38,224
and it's one nobody else can see.

507
00:16:38,574 --> 00:16:39,723
That's angle two.

508
00:16:39,903 --> 00:16:41,120
A car is a tool,

509
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:44,085
nobody's watching, wealth is invisible,

510
00:16:44,085 --> 00:16:45,530
be willing to wait,

511
00:16:45,530 --> 00:16:47,507
and get off the treadmill.

512
00:16:47,687 --> 00:16:48,828
Now angle three,

513
00:16:48,828 --> 00:16:49,754
the playbook.

514
00:16:49,934 --> 00:16:52,409
Five rules millionaires apply not just to

515
00:16:52,409 --> 00:16:53,858
cars, but to everything.

516
00:16:54,209 --> 00:16:56,542
The old car isn't really about cars.

517
00:16:56,722 --> 00:16:58,527
It's a symptom of a whole way

518
00:16:58,527 --> 00:16:59,648
of handling money.

519
00:16:59,828 --> 00:17:01,701
The same rules show up in how

520
00:17:01,701 --> 00:17:03,252
millionaires buy houses,

521
00:17:03,252 --> 00:17:06,353
clothes, electronics and almost everything else.

522
00:17:06,533 --> 00:17:08,390
Here are the five that matter most.

523
00:17:08,741 --> 00:17:10,041
Rule number eleven.

524
00:17:10,221 --> 00:17:12,613
Take care of what you already own.

525
00:17:12,963 --> 00:17:14,412
Regular oil changes,

526
00:17:14,412 --> 00:17:18,251
tire rotations and following the maintenance schedule

527
00:17:18,251 --> 00:17:20,497
can help a car last far longer.

528
00:17:20,677 --> 00:17:22,310
The same applies to homes,

529
00:17:22,310 --> 00:17:23,754
appliances and clothes.

530
00:17:23,934 --> 00:17:27,142
Small maintenance costs today often prevent big

531
00:17:27,142 --> 00:17:28,984
replacement costs tomorrow.

532
00:17:29,164 --> 00:17:32,536
Many millionaires are meticulous about maintenance for

533
00:17:32,536 --> 00:17:33,785
exactly this reason.

534
00:17:34,136 --> 00:17:36,713
Skipping maintenance is a false saving.

535
00:17:36,893 --> 00:17:39,644
A neglected car can need expensive repairs

536
00:17:39,644 --> 00:17:42,001
years earlier than a maintained one.

537
00:17:42,181 --> 00:17:44,429
A neglected roof can damage the whole

538
00:17:44,429 --> 00:17:44,794
house.

539
00:17:44,974 --> 00:17:46,862
Taking care of what you own is

540
00:17:46,862 --> 00:17:48,876
one of the most reliable ways to

541
00:17:48,876 --> 00:17:50,198
spend less over time.

542
00:17:50,548 --> 00:17:51,523
Apply the rule.

543
00:17:51,703 --> 00:17:54,199
Find your car's maintenance schedule in the

544
00:17:54,199 --> 00:17:55,882
owner's manual and follow it.

545
00:17:56,062 --> 00:17:57,529
Do the same for your home,

546
00:17:57,529 --> 00:17:59,278
with a simple yearly checklist.

547
00:17:59,458 --> 00:18:01,158
Fix small problems early,

548
00:18:01,158 --> 00:18:03,266
before they turn into big ones.

549
00:18:03,617 --> 00:18:04,847
Rule number twelve.

550
00:18:05,027 --> 00:18:07,338
Don't borrow for things that lose value.

551
00:18:07,688 --> 00:18:10,569
Many financially careful people follow a simple

552
00:18:10,569 --> 00:18:11,182
principle.

553
00:18:11,362 --> 00:18:14,224
Borrowing might make sense for things that

554
00:18:14,224 --> 00:18:16,131
can grow in value or income,

555
00:18:16,131 --> 00:18:17,971
like a reasonable mortgage,

556
00:18:17,971 --> 00:18:20,219
an education with a clear payoff,

557
00:18:20,219 --> 00:18:21,173
or a business.

558
00:18:21,353 --> 00:18:24,185
But borrowing for things that lose value,

559
00:18:24,185 --> 00:18:26,741
like cars, vacations and electronics,

560
00:18:26,741 --> 00:18:28,330
makes you poorer twice.

561
00:18:28,510 --> 00:18:30,635
You pay interest on something that's shrinking.

562
00:18:30,985 --> 00:18:33,539
Car loans are the most common example.

563
00:18:33,719 --> 00:18:35,654
You pay interest every month on an

564
00:18:35,654 --> 00:18:37,817
asset that's losing value every month.

565
00:18:37,997 --> 00:18:40,575
That's why so many millionaires prefer to

566
00:18:40,575 --> 00:18:41,706
pay cash for cars,

567
00:18:41,706 --> 00:18:43,529
or at least keep loans short,

568
00:18:43,529 --> 00:18:45,918
even when they could easily afford the

569
00:18:45,918 --> 00:18:46,484
payments.

570
00:18:46,834 --> 00:18:47,797
Apply the rule.

571
00:18:47,977 --> 00:18:49,440
Look at every loan you have.

572
00:18:49,620 --> 00:18:51,369
Is it for something that grows,

573
00:18:51,369 --> 00:18:52,836
or something that shrinks?

574
00:18:53,016 --> 00:18:55,641
Prioritize paying off the loans on shrinking

575
00:18:55,641 --> 00:18:58,147
things first, especially if they have high

576
00:18:58,147 --> 00:18:59,042
interest rates.

577
00:18:59,392 --> 00:19:00,750
Rule number thirteen.

578
00:19:00,930 --> 00:19:02,863
Insure against disasters,

579
00:19:02,863 --> 00:19:04,332
not inconveniences.

580
00:19:04,682 --> 00:19:06,952
Older cars often don't need the same

581
00:19:06,952 --> 00:19:08,339
insurance as new ones.

582
00:19:08,519 --> 00:19:10,252
Once a car's value drops,

583
00:19:10,252 --> 00:19:13,581
some owners decide that collision coverage costs

584
00:19:13,581 --> 00:19:15,037
more than it's worth,

585
00:19:15,037 --> 00:19:17,672
especially if they have the savings to

586
00:19:17,672 --> 00:19:18,782
replace the car.

587
00:19:18,962 --> 00:19:22,278
Raising deductibles, when you have an emergency

588
00:19:22,278 --> 00:19:24,395
fund, can also lower premiums.

589
00:19:24,746 --> 00:19:27,738
The bigger principle is that wealthy people

590
00:19:27,738 --> 00:19:30,730
insure against losses they couldn't handle,

591
00:19:30,730 --> 00:19:33,305
like liability in a serious accident,

592
00:19:33,305 --> 00:19:35,601
while self insuring small losses.

593
00:19:35,781 --> 00:19:38,910
That's why liability coverage stays important even

594
00:19:38,910 --> 00:19:39,786
on an old car.

595
00:19:39,966 --> 00:19:42,306
A serious accident can lead to costs

596
00:19:42,306 --> 00:19:44,320
far bigger than the car itself.

597
00:19:44,670 --> 00:19:45,656
Apply the rule.

598
00:19:45,836 --> 00:19:48,054
Check your car insurance this year.

599
00:19:48,234 --> 00:19:51,065
Compare what you pay for collision coverage

600
00:19:51,065 --> 00:19:53,435
with what the car is actually worth.

601
00:19:53,615 --> 00:19:55,868
Make sure your liability limits are high

602
00:19:55,868 --> 00:19:56,262
enough.

603
00:19:56,442 --> 00:19:58,647
And shop around for quotes at least

604
00:19:58,647 --> 00:19:59,403
once a year.

605
00:19:59,753 --> 00:20:01,250
Rule number fourteen.

606
00:20:01,430 --> 00:20:03,404
Make good decisions automatic.

607
00:20:03,754 --> 00:20:06,819
Millionaires who drive old cars aren't superhuman.

608
00:20:06,999 --> 00:20:08,871
Many have simply set up systems so

609
00:20:08,871 --> 00:20:11,074
the right choices happen without effort.

610
00:20:11,254 --> 00:20:14,249
Automatic transfers to retirement accounts,

611
00:20:14,249 --> 00:20:16,687
automatic payments into a car fund,

612
00:20:16,687 --> 00:20:19,613
automatic bill payments so late fees never

613
00:20:19,613 --> 00:20:20,101
happen.

614
00:20:20,451 --> 00:20:22,829
Automation protects you from your own worst

615
00:20:22,829 --> 00:20:23,272
moments.

616
00:20:23,452 --> 00:20:25,534
If money is moved to investments on

617
00:20:25,534 --> 00:20:28,092
payday, there's less sitting in checking to

618
00:20:28,092 --> 00:20:29,698
be tempted by a new car ad.

619
00:20:29,878 --> 00:20:31,597
The default becomes saving,

620
00:20:31,597 --> 00:20:34,081
and spending becomes the active choice.

621
00:20:34,431 --> 00:20:35,441
Apply the rule.

622
00:20:35,621 --> 00:20:38,871
This week, set up three automatic transfers.

623
00:20:39,051 --> 00:20:40,460
One to retirement,

624
00:20:40,460 --> 00:20:42,415
one to an emergency fund,

625
00:20:42,415 --> 00:20:44,137
and one to a car fund.

626
00:20:44,317 --> 00:20:45,280
Start small.

627
00:20:45,460 --> 00:20:47,144
You can always increase them later.

628
00:20:47,494 --> 00:20:49,061
And rule number fifteen.

629
00:20:49,241 --> 00:20:51,341
Spend lavishly on what you love,

630
00:20:51,341 --> 00:20:53,769
and cut ruthlessly on what you don't.

631
00:20:54,119 --> 00:20:57,093
Personal finance author Ramit Sethi calls this

632
00:20:57,093 --> 00:20:58,321
conscious spending.

633
00:20:58,501 --> 00:21:00,152
The idea is that being good with

634
00:21:00,152 --> 00:21:02,112
money doesn't mean cutting everything.

635
00:21:02,292 --> 00:21:04,817
It means deciding what you truly value,

636
00:21:04,817 --> 00:21:06,371
spending freely on that,

637
00:21:06,371 --> 00:21:09,156
and cutting costs mercilessly on things you

638
00:21:09,156 --> 00:21:10,257
don't care about.

639
00:21:10,607 --> 00:21:13,732
That's exactly why so many millionaires drive

640
00:21:13,732 --> 00:21:14,357
old cars.

641
00:21:14,537 --> 00:21:16,014
They don't care about cars,

642
00:21:16,014 --> 00:21:17,930
so they spend as little as possible

643
00:21:17,930 --> 00:21:18,368
on them.

644
00:21:18,548 --> 00:21:21,297
But many spend generously on things they

645
00:21:21,297 --> 00:21:22,603
value, like travel,

646
00:21:22,603 --> 00:21:24,115
their kids' education,

647
00:21:24,115 --> 00:21:25,421
charity or a hobby.

648
00:21:25,601 --> 00:21:27,064
Their spending isn't small.

649
00:21:27,244 --> 00:21:28,207
It's focused.

650
00:21:28,387 --> 00:21:29,787
And because it's focused,

651
00:21:29,787 --> 00:21:32,251
it actually makes them happier than spending

652
00:21:32,251 --> 00:21:34,378
a little bit on everything ever could.

653
00:21:34,729 --> 00:21:35,739
Apply the rule.

654
00:21:35,919 --> 00:21:38,211
Write down three things you truly love

655
00:21:38,211 --> 00:21:39,297
spending money on.

656
00:21:39,477 --> 00:21:41,593
Then write down three things you spend

657
00:21:41,593 --> 00:21:43,320
on but don't really care about.

658
00:21:43,500 --> 00:21:44,766
Cut the second list,

659
00:21:44,766 --> 00:21:46,855
and feel free to enjoy the first.

660
00:21:47,035 --> 00:21:48,301
That's not being cheap.

661
00:21:48,481 --> 00:21:49,781
It's being intentional.

662
00:21:50,131 --> 00:21:51,779
Now let's make this practical.

663
00:21:51,959 --> 00:21:53,768
Here's how to buy your next car

664
00:21:53,768 --> 00:21:55,752
the way a quiet millionaire would,

665
00:21:55,752 --> 00:21:56,510
step by step.

666
00:21:56,861 --> 00:21:57,569
Step one.

667
00:21:57,749 --> 00:22:00,000
Decide your budget before you shop,

668
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,316
based on the full cost of ownership,

669
00:22:02,316 --> 00:22:03,345
not the payment.

670
00:22:03,525 --> 00:22:06,221
A rule of thumb many personal finance

671
00:22:06,221 --> 00:22:08,625
writers use is to keep your total

672
00:22:08,625 --> 00:22:11,030
vehicle costs, including payment,

673
00:22:11,030 --> 00:22:12,414
insurance and fuel,

674
00:22:12,414 --> 00:22:14,600
to a modest share of your take

675
00:22:14,600 --> 00:22:17,442
home pay, often suggested as around ten

676
00:22:17,442 --> 00:22:18,827
to fifteen percent.

677
00:22:19,007 --> 00:22:21,088
Write the number down before you ever

678
00:22:21,088 --> 00:22:22,100
set foot on a lot,

679
00:22:22,100 --> 00:22:24,574
because dealerships are designed to make you

680
00:22:24,574 --> 00:22:25,137
forget it.

681
00:22:25,487 --> 00:22:26,404
Step two.

682
00:22:26,584 --> 00:22:28,871
Pick models known for reliability.

683
00:22:29,051 --> 00:22:31,923
Look at reliability ratings from sources like

684
00:22:31,923 --> 00:22:34,986
Consumer Reports, and check crash safety ratings

685
00:22:34,986 --> 00:22:38,049
from the Insurance Institute for Highway Safety.

686
00:22:38,229 --> 00:22:41,236
Reliable mainstream models tend to hold their

687
00:22:41,236 --> 00:22:43,775
value reasonably well and cost less to

688
00:22:43,775 --> 00:22:44,243
repair.

689
00:22:44,423 --> 00:22:46,869
A boring car with a great reliability

690
00:22:46,869 --> 00:22:49,513
record is often the smartest purchase on

691
00:22:49,513 --> 00:22:50,042
the lot.

692
00:22:50,392 --> 00:22:51,285
Step three.

693
00:22:51,465 --> 00:22:53,811
Check the history and get an inspection.

694
00:22:53,991 --> 00:22:56,456
Run a vehicle history report to check

695
00:22:56,456 --> 00:22:59,587
for accidents, flood damage and title problems.

696
00:22:59,767 --> 00:23:02,558
Then pay an independent mechanic to inspect

697
00:23:02,558 --> 00:23:04,051
the car before you buy.

698
00:23:04,231 --> 00:23:06,433
It usually costs a modest fee and

699
00:23:06,433 --> 00:23:08,701
can save you from a very expensive

700
00:23:08,701 --> 00:23:09,235
mistake.

701
00:23:09,415 --> 00:23:11,898
Sellers who refuse an inspection are telling

702
00:23:11,898 --> 00:23:12,689
you something.

703
00:23:13,039 --> 00:23:14,002
Step four.

704
00:23:14,182 --> 00:23:16,269
Arrange financing before you shop,

705
00:23:16,269 --> 00:23:17,189
if you need it.

706
00:23:17,369 --> 00:23:19,884
Credit unions and banks often offer better

707
00:23:19,884 --> 00:23:21,560
rates than dealer financing.

708
00:23:21,740 --> 00:23:23,585
With a pre approval in hand,

709
00:23:23,585 --> 00:23:26,022
you can negotiate on the total price,

710
00:23:26,022 --> 00:23:27,603
not the monthly payment.

711
00:23:27,783 --> 00:23:28,770
Step five.

712
00:23:28,950 --> 00:23:31,231
Negotiate the out the door price,

713
00:23:31,231 --> 00:23:33,304
which includes taxes and fees.

714
00:23:33,484 --> 00:23:34,889
And be ready to walk away.

715
00:23:35,069 --> 00:23:36,845
There are always more cars.

716
00:23:37,195 --> 00:23:38,367
Step six.

717
00:23:38,547 --> 00:23:40,107
Say no to the extras in the

718
00:23:40,107 --> 00:23:40,974
finance office.

719
00:23:41,154 --> 00:23:43,723
Extended warranties, paint protection,

720
00:23:43,723 --> 00:23:46,427
fabric protection, and other add ons are

721
00:23:46,427 --> 00:23:48,050
often heavily marked up.

722
00:23:48,230 --> 00:23:49,593
If you want a warranty,

723
00:23:49,593 --> 00:23:52,200
you can research options separately and take

724
00:23:52,200 --> 00:23:52,793
your time.

725
00:23:52,973 --> 00:23:54,087
And step seven.

726
00:23:54,267 --> 00:23:55,559
Once you drive home,

727
00:23:55,559 --> 00:23:57,625
start your car fund immediately,

728
00:23:57,625 --> 00:23:59,368
so the next car is paid for

729
00:23:59,368 --> 00:24:00,595
before you need it.

730
00:24:00,945 --> 00:24:02,022
In your twenties,

731
00:24:02,022 --> 00:24:03,670
the car rule matters most,

732
00:24:03,670 --> 00:24:05,889
because every dollar not spent on a

733
00:24:05,889 --> 00:24:07,980
car can compound for forty years.

734
00:24:08,160 --> 00:24:09,653
A reliable used car,

735
00:24:09,653 --> 00:24:11,744
paid in cash or with a short

736
00:24:11,744 --> 00:24:14,283
loan, is one of the best financial

737
00:24:14,283 --> 00:24:16,821
decisions a young person can make.

738
00:24:17,001 --> 00:24:19,875
Many people who become millionaires look back

739
00:24:19,875 --> 00:24:21,854
on driving a cheap car in their

740
00:24:21,854 --> 00:24:23,642
twenties as a turning point.

741
00:24:23,993 --> 00:24:25,816
In your thirties and forties,

742
00:24:25,816 --> 00:24:27,325
family needs often grow.

743
00:24:27,505 --> 00:24:29,687
Safety and space matter more.

744
00:24:29,867 --> 00:24:30,494
That's fine.

745
00:24:30,674 --> 00:24:32,137
The same rules apply.

746
00:24:32,317 --> 00:24:33,745
Buy a few years old,

747
00:24:33,745 --> 00:24:36,100
choose reliability, keep it long,

748
00:24:36,100 --> 00:24:37,600
and avoid long loans.

749
00:24:37,780 --> 00:24:39,995
A minivan you keep for twelve years

750
00:24:39,995 --> 00:24:42,400
can be a fantastic financial decision.

751
00:24:42,580 --> 00:24:45,662
Two car households can also stagger purchases

752
00:24:45,662 --> 00:24:48,333
so they're never replacing both cars at

753
00:24:48,333 --> 00:24:48,676
once.

754
00:24:49,026 --> 00:24:50,854
In your fifties and beyond,

755
00:24:50,854 --> 00:24:53,765
many people finally have the financial room

756
00:24:53,765 --> 00:24:55,051
to buy a nicer car.

757
00:24:55,231 --> 00:24:57,124
If your retirement is on track,

758
00:24:57,124 --> 00:24:58,955
a car you truly enjoy might be

759
00:24:58,955 --> 00:25:00,908
part of your conscious spending.

760
00:25:01,088 --> 00:25:03,462
Just remember that every car is still

761
00:25:03,462 --> 00:25:04,810
a depreciating asset,

762
00:25:04,810 --> 00:25:06,991
and that money spent on cars close

763
00:25:06,991 --> 00:25:09,622
to retirement has fewer years to recover.

764
00:25:09,972 --> 00:25:11,844
Let's look at the car mistakes that

765
00:25:11,844 --> 00:25:12,967
cost people the most.

766
00:25:13,147 --> 00:25:14,018
Mistake one.

767
00:25:14,198 --> 00:25:16,729
Rolling negative equity into a new loan.

768
00:25:16,909 --> 00:25:18,595
If you owe more than your car

769
00:25:18,595 --> 00:25:20,049
is worth and trade it in,

770
00:25:20,049 --> 00:25:22,317
the dealer may add the leftover balance

771
00:25:22,317 --> 00:25:23,306
to your new loan.

772
00:25:23,486 --> 00:25:25,986
You start the new car already underwater,

773
00:25:25,986 --> 00:25:27,877
paying interest on a car you no

774
00:25:27,877 --> 00:25:28,548
longer own.

775
00:25:28,898 --> 00:25:30,012
Mistake two.

776
00:25:30,192 --> 00:25:31,829
Shopping by monthly payment.

777
00:25:32,009 --> 00:25:34,276
When the only question is what payment

778
00:25:34,276 --> 00:25:35,170
you can handle,

779
00:25:35,170 --> 00:25:36,959
a seller can stretch the loan,

780
00:25:36,959 --> 00:25:38,808
add extras and raise the price,

781
00:25:38,808 --> 00:25:41,135
all while keeping the payment the same.

782
00:25:41,315 --> 00:25:42,336
Mistake three.

783
00:25:42,516 --> 00:25:43,700
Leasing forever.

784
00:25:43,880 --> 00:25:46,424
Leasing can make sense in some situations,

785
00:25:46,424 --> 00:25:48,787
but leasing one car after another means

786
00:25:48,787 --> 00:25:50,907
you always have a payment and never

787
00:25:50,907 --> 00:25:51,694
own anything.

788
00:25:52,043 --> 00:25:53,030
Mistake four.

789
00:25:53,210 --> 00:25:55,230
Buying more car than your life needs.

790
00:25:55,410 --> 00:25:57,541
A large truck or SUV for a

791
00:25:57,541 --> 00:26:00,164
solo commute costs more in fuel,

792
00:26:00,164 --> 00:26:02,376
insurance and depreciation.

793
00:26:02,556 --> 00:26:04,432
Buy for the life you actually live,

794
00:26:04,432 --> 00:26:06,039
not the one in the commercial.

795
00:26:06,219 --> 00:26:07,439
And mistake five.

796
00:26:07,619 --> 00:26:10,060
Skipping maintenance to save money,

797
00:26:10,060 --> 00:26:12,432
which often turns a cheap fix into

798
00:26:12,432 --> 00:26:13,827
an expensive repair,

799
00:26:13,827 --> 00:26:16,129
and shortens the life of the car.

800
00:26:16,479 --> 00:26:17,709
Here's the bigger lesson.

801
00:26:17,889 --> 00:26:20,004
The old car rule works for almost

802
00:26:20,004 --> 00:26:21,221
everything you buy.

803
00:26:21,401 --> 00:26:22,075
Houses.

804
00:26:22,255 --> 00:26:23,445
Buy what you need,

805
00:26:23,445 --> 00:26:24,503
take care of it,

806
00:26:24,503 --> 00:26:26,686
and stay long enough to avoid the

807
00:26:26,686 --> 00:26:28,803
costs of moving again and again.

808
00:26:28,983 --> 00:26:29,714
Phones.

809
00:26:29,894 --> 00:26:32,234
Keep them until they genuinely stop working

810
00:26:32,234 --> 00:26:32,506
well.

811
00:26:32,686 --> 00:26:33,429
Clothes.

812
00:26:33,609 --> 00:26:35,348
Buy quality where it matters,

813
00:26:35,348 --> 00:26:36,427
wear it for years,

814
00:26:36,427 --> 00:26:37,626
and skip the trends.

815
00:26:37,977 --> 00:26:39,939
The pattern is always the same.

816
00:26:40,119 --> 00:26:41,943
Buy something good enough,

817
00:26:41,943 --> 00:26:43,346
often slightly used.

818
00:26:43,526 --> 00:26:44,525
Take care of it.

819
00:26:44,705 --> 00:26:46,859
Keep it much longer than most people

820
00:26:46,859 --> 00:26:47,038
do.

821
00:26:47,218 --> 00:26:49,270
And invest the money you didn't spend

822
00:26:49,270 --> 00:26:49,935
on upgrades.

823
00:26:50,115 --> 00:26:51,949
Do that across your whole life,

824
00:26:51,949 --> 00:26:53,842
and the results can be enormous,

825
00:26:53,842 --> 00:26:56,032
because you're not just saving on one

826
00:26:56,032 --> 00:26:56,268
car.

827
00:26:56,448 --> 00:26:59,444
You're saving on hundreds of purchases over

828
00:26:59,444 --> 00:27:00,001
decades.

829
00:27:00,351 --> 00:27:02,697
That's really why rich people drive old

830
00:27:02,697 --> 00:27:02,998
cars.

831
00:27:03,178 --> 00:27:04,571
It's not one decision.

832
00:27:04,751 --> 00:27:07,018
It's a philosophy that shows up everywhere

833
00:27:07,018 --> 00:27:07,827
in their lives.

834
00:27:08,007 --> 00:27:09,807
The car is just the most visible

835
00:27:09,807 --> 00:27:12,282
example, because it's sitting right there in

836
00:27:12,282 --> 00:27:14,138
the driveway for everyone to see.

837
00:27:14,488 --> 00:27:16,295
Let me give you a few real

838
00:27:16,295 --> 00:27:19,145
examples of wealthy people who are famous

839
00:27:19,145 --> 00:27:20,258
for modest cars.

840
00:27:20,608 --> 00:27:23,096
Warren Buffett, one of the richest people

841
00:27:23,096 --> 00:27:23,885
in the world,

842
00:27:23,885 --> 00:27:26,069
has been known for years for driving

843
00:27:26,069 --> 00:27:28,315
ordinary cars and keeping them a long

844
00:27:28,315 --> 00:27:28,618
time.

845
00:27:28,798 --> 00:27:30,863
He has joked in interviews that he

846
00:27:30,863 --> 00:27:32,927
only drives a few thousand miles a

847
00:27:32,927 --> 00:27:34,749
year, so he doesn't need a new

848
00:27:34,749 --> 00:27:35,660
car very often.

849
00:27:35,840 --> 00:27:37,688
For a man worth well over one

850
00:27:37,688 --> 00:27:39,217
hundred billion dollars,

851
00:27:39,217 --> 00:27:41,192
the car simply isn't important.

852
00:27:41,542 --> 00:27:43,497
And then there are the quiet millionaires

853
00:27:43,497 --> 00:27:44,641
you'll never hear about.

854
00:27:44,821 --> 00:27:47,018
The retired teacher with a paid off

855
00:27:47,018 --> 00:27:49,152
house and a twelve year old Camry.

856
00:27:49,332 --> 00:27:51,361
The plumber who owns his business and

857
00:27:51,361 --> 00:27:53,335
drives a work truck with two hundred

858
00:27:53,335 --> 00:27:54,487
thousand miles on it.

859
00:27:54,667 --> 00:27:56,825
The engineer who bought a three year

860
00:27:56,825 --> 00:27:58,864
old minivan and drove it until her

861
00:27:58,864 --> 00:28:00,123
kids went to college.

862
00:28:00,303 --> 00:28:02,777
Stanley's research is full of people like

863
00:28:02,777 --> 00:28:03,078
them.

864
00:28:03,429 --> 00:28:05,507
None of them are depriving themselves.

865
00:28:05,687 --> 00:28:07,721
They still enjoy road trips,

866
00:28:07,721 --> 00:28:10,191
